<h1><span class="nowrap">ro-drums-909</span> user guide</h1>
<p class="lede">ro-drums-909 runs the TR-909's own sequencer program: what you know from a TR-909 applies here, and the procedures below are the owner's manual's. Installing is on the <a href="/download#installing">download page</a>; the ROM folder, artwork files, the update check and contact are on <a href="/support">Support</a>.</p>
<ol class="toc">
{toc.map(({ id, title }) => <li><a href={`#${id}`}>{title}</a></li>)}
</ol>
</header>

<!-- ===================================================================== -->
<section id="start">
<div class="eyebrow">1</div>
<h2>Getting started</h2>

<h3 id="first-start">The first start</h3>
<p>The sequencer program and the hi-hat's, crash's and ride's samples are in ROMs, Roland's copyrighted work, so Lemus doesn't include them. The first time ro-drums-909 starts without its program ROM, it asks how to continue.</p>
<Shot src={prompt} base={base} alt="The first-start prompt over the dimmed panel: the five ROMs and the artwork not found, where Lemus looked, and two choices, I have the ROM with Locate ROM… and Use voice mode with what voice mode keeps and loses" crop={{ x: 314, y: 8, w: 588, h: 756 }} caption="The prompt the first time ro-drums-909 starts without its ROMs." />
<ul>
<li><span class="k">I have the ROM</span> (Locate ROM…) takes a ROM image, a zip holding them such as MAME's tr909.zip, or a folder. A file is checked and copied into the ROM folder; a folder becomes the ROM folder. The full TR-909 starts at once, settings kept.</li>
<li><span class="k">Use voice mode</span> carries on without it.</li>
</ul>
<p>The prompt doesn't come back, unless the program ROM goes missing after ro-drums-909 has run with it. In voice mode the top bar keeps <span class="k">Add Program ROM…</span>.</p>

<h3 id="roms">The ROMs</h3>
<p>The five images in MAME's tr909 set, each found by checksum anywhere in the <a href="/support#rom">ROM folder</a>. All are optional.</p>
<div class="scroll">
<table>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">File</th><th scope="col">What</th><th scope="col">Without it</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td class="mono">tr909_v4.ic609</td><td>The sequencer program, version 4.0 (IC609, 8 KB)</td><td><a href="#voice-mode">Voice mode</a></td></tr>
<tr><td class="mono">hn61256p__c43.ic69</td><td>The hi-hat's samples (IC69, 32 KB)</td><td>No hi-hats, closed or open</td></tr>
<tr><td class="mono">hn61256p__c42.ic62</td><td>The crash's (IC62, 32 KB)</td><td>No crash</td></tr>
<tr><td class="mono">hn61256p__c44.ic54</td><td>The ride's (IC54, 32 KB)</td><td>No ride</td></tr>
<tr><td class="mono">tr909_v1.ic609</td><td>The sequencer program, version 1.0 (8 KB): it makes the factory patterns</td><td>No <a href="#factory">factory patterns</a></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div class="card">
<div class="label">The ROMs Lemus looks for</div>
<p class="mono">tr909_v4.ic609 · 8,192 bytes · CRC a9b45f30<br>SHA1 b94374e2cd6b5a6c405da4fa7793c98c0a6015c4</p>
<p class="mono">hn61256p__c43.ic69 · 32,768 bytes · CRC 2aaae11b<br>SHA1 22a34d603e78673bb096f5e56a8971afe14d8fee</p>
<p class="mono">hn61256p__c42.ic62 · 32,768 bytes · CRC dc61cf1a<br>SHA1 bc730107b0c391f4879c9777a3e85f3e57f3aad6</p>
<p class="mono">hn61256p__c44.ic54 · 32,768 bytes · CRC 01a9b435<br>SHA1 daa54c58c7e3ae3398f125568537ec82d5bd1dfd</p>
<p class="mono">tr909_v1.ic609 · 8,192 bytes · CRC bfb9682b<br>SHA1 bcd754900574d1861034af8056574ffa77db3067</p>
</div>

<h3 id="voice-mode">Voice mode</h3>
<p>Without the program ROM, ro-drums-909 is the TR-909's voicing board, played from MIDI: the same sound, without anything the CPU does.</p>
<div class="scroll">
<table>
<thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">With the program ROM</th><th scope="col">Voice mode</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">Sound</th><td>The voicing board</td><td>The same</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">MIDI notes</th><td>Through the firmware's MIDI IN</td><td>Straight to the voices, with the firmware's velocity laws</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Knobs, controllers, automation</th><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Sequencer and display</th><td>The original firmware</td><td>No</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Panel keys and LEDs</th><td>Work as on the unit</td><td>No effect</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">SYNC, MIDI out</th><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>A session keeps its pattern memory in voice mode. Adding the program ROM later switches it to the full machine, keeping its knobs and memory. Without a sample ROM, its voice is silent, and the status line says which: <span class="k">no HI HAT/CRASH/RIDE ROM</span>.</p>

<h3 id="add-rom">Adding ROMs later</h3>
<Shot src={roms} base={base} alt="The ROMs pop-up under the top bar in voice mode: the ROM folder's path with Show, Change… and Rescan, the five ROMs not found with their checksums, and the artwork" crop={{ x: 540, y: 0, w: 675, h: 625 }} marks={[{ x: 920, y: 3, w: 146, h: 26, n: 1 }]} caption="Add Program ROM… in the top bar, while ro-drums-909 is in voice mode." />
<ol>
<li>Click <span class="k">Add Program ROM…</span> <Num n="1" /> in the top bar (with the program ROM in place, the same button is <span class="k">ROMs</span>).</li>
<li>Put the ROMs in the folder shown, or choose your own with <span class="k">Change…</span>.</li>
<li>Click <span class="k">Rescan</span>. ro-drums-909 reloads with them.</li>
</ol>

<h3 id="factory">The factory patterns</h3>
<p>A new TR-909 held a demonstration in Bank I: patterns 1 to 8 of pattern group 1, and track 1, 65 measures from pattern 5, with a stored tempo of 110 BPM. Version 4.0 of the program dropped it. With <span class="mono">tr909_v1.ic609</span> in the ROM folder, Lemus makes that memory as the service notes say, by starting the 1.0 program with TRACK 1 and PATTERN 1 held, and plays it on 4.0.</p>
<ul>
<li>A new instance holds them, and so does State: Reset. Without the 1.0 ROM, a new instance's memory is empty.</li>
<li>START plays track 1. TEMPO + TRACK 1 selects it at its stored 110 BPM.</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="artwork">Panel artwork</h3>
<p>The four blank places take an SVG or PNG of your own each, named as below, in <span class="path">artwork/tr909/</span> in the ROM folder (<a href="/support#artwork">how artwork files work</a>). <span class="k">ROMs</span> in the top bar lists them with what each has, opens the folder with Show its folder, and reads it again with Rescan.</p>
<Shot src={panel} base={base} alt="The top of the panel with the three blank artwork places outlined: maker at top left, model under it, name at the right" crop={upper} marks={[{ x: 87, y: 68, w: 201, h: 32, n: 'maker' }, { x: 87, y: 219, w: 270, h: 39, n: 'model' }, { x: 765, y: 241, w: 339, h: 17, n: 'name' }]} caption="The three places on the top panel." />
<Shot src={rear} base={base} alt="The rear panel with the blank place of its Roland logo outlined, between MIDI IN and the AC inlet" crop={{ x: 0, y: 40, w: 1215, h: 280 }} marks={[{ x: 756, y: 216, w: 275, h: 55, n: 'rear_maker' }]} caption="The place on the rear panel (REAR in the bottom bar)." />
<div class="scroll">
<table>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">File</th><th scope="col">Place</th><th scope="col">Size</th><th scope="col">A sharp PNG</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td class="mono">maker.svg or .png</td><td>Roland logo</td><td>80.5 × 12.6 mm</td><td>805 × 126 px</td></tr>
<tr><td class="mono">model.svg or .png</td><td>TR-909</td><td>107.9 × 15.6 mm</td><td>1079 × 156 px</td></tr>
<tr><td class="mono">name.svg or .png</td><td>Rhythm Composer</td><td>135.5 × 6.9 mm</td><td>1355 × 69 px</td></tr>
<tr><td class="mono">rear_maker.svg or .png</td><td>Roland logo, rear panel</td><td>110.0 × 22.0 mm</td><td>1100 × 220 px</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<ul>
<li><span class="mono">artwork/tr909.zip</span> holding the same files works too.</li>
<li>A <span class="mono">tr909.svg</span> still fills the places without a file: its elements <span class="mono">roland</span>, <span class="mono">tr909</span> and <span class="mono">rhythm_composer</span>, in panel millimetres, filled in the print colour. The rear place takes its <span class="mono">roland</span>, in the rear's grey.</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="first-sound">Your first sound</h3>
<ul>
<li><span class="k">From MIDI</span>, in both modes: see <a href="#midi-notes">MIDI notes</a>.</li>
<li><span class="k">From the sequencer</span>, with the program ROM: with the factory patterns, START plays track 1. Without them the memory is empty, as on a new unit with version 4.0: <a href="#write">write a pattern</a> first. With SYNC: HOST, START does nothing: the DAW's transport runs it.</li>
</ul>

</section>

<!-- ===================================================================== -->
<section id="panel">
<div class="eyebrow">2</div>
<h2>The panel at a glance</h2>
<p>Drawn from photos of the original and the service notes' board artwork, to within about 0.3 mm.</p>
<Shot src={panel} base={base} alt="The whole ro-drums-909 window with its controls numbered 1 to 16" marks={[
  { ...at.accent, n: 1 }, { ...at.voices, n: 2 }, { ...at.startStop, n: 3 }, { ...at.display, n: 4 }, { ...at.tempo, n: 5 }, { ...at.track, n: 6 },
  { ...at.pattern, n: 7 }, { ...at.fn4, n: 8 }, { ...at.cycle, n: 9 }, { ...at.enter, n: 10 }, { ...at.volume, n: 11 }, { ...at.fnBlock, n: 12 },
  { ...at.scaleRows, n: 13 }, { ...at.steps, n: 14 }, { ...at.legends, n: 15 }, { ...at.bar, n: 16 },
]} />
<dl class="legend">
<div><dt><Num n="1" /> TOTAL ACCENT</dt><dd>How much a step with TOTAL ACCENT raises every voice on it, read as the step plays.</dd></div>
<div><dt><Num n="2" /> Voice knobs</dt><dd>BASS DRUM: TUNE, LEVEL, ATTACK, DECAY. SNARE DRUM: TUNE, LEVEL, TONE, SNAPPY. Each tom: TUNE, LEVEL, DECAY. RIM SHOT and HAND CLAP: LEVEL. HI HAT: LEVEL, CH DECAY, OH DECAY. CYMBAL: CRASH and RIDE, LEVEL and TUNE. All 0 to 10; the LEVELs start at 7.5, the others at 5.</dd></div>
<div><dt><Num n="3" /> START, STOP/CONT</dt><dd>START plays from the top. STOP/CONT stops, and pressed again continues where it stopped. Neither works in MIDI sync.</dd></div>
<div><dt><Num n="4" /> MEAS/TEMPO</dt><dd>The measure of a track; the tempo while TEMPO is held; the measures left while AVAILABLE MEAS is.</dd></div>
<div><dt><Num n="5" /> TEMPO</dt><dd>36 to 290 BPM, about 120 by default. Ignored in MIDI sync, and while a track's <a href="#tempo-memory">stored tempo</a> is in use.</dd></div>
<div><dt><Num n="6" /> TRACK PLAY 1–4</dt><dd>Selects a track of the bank. With SHIFT: TRACK WRITE.</dd></div>
<div><dt><Num n="7" /> PATTERN PLAY 1–3, EXT INST</dt><dd>Selects a pattern group; a step key then picks the pattern. With SHIFT: PATTERN WRITE in that group, and EXT INST switches the step keys to the <a href="#ext-inst">external instruments</a>.</dd></div>
<div><dt><Num n="8" /> TEMPO, BACK, FWD, AVAILABLE MEAS</dt><dd>TEMPO shows the tempo and stores it in a track; BACK and FWD step through a track's measures; AVAILABLE MEAS shows the measures left. With SHIFT: STEP and TAP, the pattern write modes; BANK I and II.</dd></div>
<div><dt><Num n="9" /> CYCLE/GUIDE, TAPE SYNC</dt><dd>CYCLE repeats a track; in tap write, GUIDE is the metronome. TAPE SYNC makes START wait for a tape's sync tone: there is no tape input, so nothing plays until STOP/CONT leaves it. With SHIFT: LAST MEAS, and TEMPO MODE.</dd></div>
<div><dt><Num n="10" /> CARTRIDGE/ENTER</dt><dd>ENTER for measure numbers, chains, copies and track writing. With INSTRUMENT SELECT: TOTAL ACCENT. No cartridge is emulated: the 909 finds none inserted.</dd></div>
<div><dt><Num n="11" /> VOLUME</dt><dd>The master outputs' level, 7 by default.</dd></div>
<div><dt><Num n="12" /> LAST STEP, SCALE, SHUFFLE/FLAM, CLEAR, INSTRUMENT SELECT, SHIFT</dt><dd>SCALE steps the pattern's scale. The others are held while another key is pressed.</dd></div>
<div><dt><Num n="13" /> Scales</dt><dd>16th, 32nd, 8th triplet and 16th triplet; the lamp at the left shows the pattern's.</dd></div>
<div><dt><Num n="14" /> Step keys 1–16</dt><dd>Write steps, and pick a pattern, a voice, a shuffle or flam setting, a last step or a MIDI channel. Their LEDs show the steps: dim for a normal note, bright for an accent.</dd></div>
<div><dt><Num n="15" /> Under the step keys</dt><dd>The digits 1 to 9 and 0 for measure numbers; COPY, INS and DEL for editing; SAVE, VERIFY and LOAD for the tape; and which keys write which voice.</dd></div>
<div><dt><Num n="16" /> Bottom bar</dt><dd>SYNC, REAR and SERVICE: see <a href="#bottom-bar">the bottom bar</a>.</dd></div>
</dl>
<p>SAVE runs with no tape. VERIFY and LOAD wait for a tape's pilot tone that never comes, MIDI frozen meanwhile: step key 1 cancels, the memory untouched.</p>
</section>

