<section id="sequencer">
<div class="eyebrow">3</div>
<h2>The sequencer</h2>

<h3 id="organised">How it's organised</h3>
<ul>
<li><span class="k">2 banks,</span> I and II, each of 3 pattern groups of 16 patterns: 48 patterns a bank.</li>
<li><span class="k">A pattern</span> has 1 to 16 steps at one of four scales. It holds the eleven voices (BD, SD, the toms and the closed hat with normal and accent, BD to HT with flam; RS, HC, the open hat, crash and ride on or off), TOTAL ACCENT for each step, 16 external instruments, and its shuffle and flam.</li>
<li><span class="k">4 tracks</span> a bank, 896 measures in all, one pattern a measure, each track with a stored tempo: see <a href="#tracks">Tracks</a>.</li>
<li>The memory is saved with the session. There is no clearing it all, as on the unit: State: Reset makes a new instance, the factory patterns with it.</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="two-keys">Two keys at once</h3>
<ul>
<li>A key that modifies another (SHIFT, INSTRUMENT SELECT, CLEAR, SHUFFLE/FLAM, LAST STEP) goes down first: pressed in the same scan, the other key may act unmodified.</li>
<li>A PATTERN PLAY key and the pattern's step key are pressed one after the other, not together; TRACK PLAY too.</li>
<li>Right-click (ctrl-click on a Mac) a key to hold it until it's clicked again; the status bar names it. The computer's Shift holds SHIFT, and the other <a href="#keys">computer keys</a> stay down while held.</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="play">Play patterns</h3>
<Shot src={panel} base={base} alt="The PATTERN PLAY keys, the step keys, and START and STOP/CONT outlined" crop={lower} marks={[{ ...at.pattern, n: 1 }, { ...at.steps, n: 2 }, { ...at.startStop, n: 3 }]} />
<ol>
<li>Press a PATTERN PLAY key <Num n="1" /> for the group, then a step key <Num n="2" /> for the pattern.</li>
<li>Press START <Num n="3" />. STOP/CONT stops it; pressed again, it continues where it stopped.</li>
</ol>
<ul>
<li>A pattern chosen while one plays starts at the next bar.</li>
<li>Two step keys pressed together play the patterns between them in turn.</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="write">Write a pattern (step write)</h3>
<Shot src={panel} base={base} alt="SHIFT, the PATTERN PLAY keys, CLEAR, START, INSTRUMENT SELECT, the step keys and the voice legends under them outlined" crop={lower} marks={[{ ...at.shift, n: 1 }, { ...at.pattern, n: 2 }, { ...at.clear, n: 3 }, { ...at.start, n: 4 }, { ...at.inst, n: 5 }, { ...at.steps, n: 6 }, { ...at.voiceLegends, n: 7 }]} />
<ol>
<li>Stopped, hold SHIFT <Num n="1" /> and press a PATTERN PLAY key <Num n="2" />. Its LED blinks: pattern write, in that group.</li>
<li>Hold CLEAR <Num n="3" /> and press a step key: that pattern is erased and selected.</li>
<li>Press START <Num n="4" />. The step LEDs run.</li>
<li>Hold INSTRUMENT SELECT <Num n="5" /> and press a key of the voice to write, as the legends under the keys show <Num n="7" />.</li>
<li>Press the step keys <Num n="6" /> to write it.</li>
</ol>
<ul>
<li><span class="k">BD, SD, the toms and the closed hat:</span> a step key goes none, normal, accent, none, with either key of the voice. The LED is dim for normal, bright for an accent.</li>
<li><span class="k">RS, HC, the open hat, crash and ride:</span> on or off. The open hat is the two hi-hat keys together.</li>
<li><span class="k">Flam:</span> INSTRUMENT SELECT with both keys of BD, SD or a tom selects its flam for writing.</li>
<li><span class="k">TOTAL ACCENT:</span> INSTRUMENT SELECT with ENTER selects it for writing.</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="accents">Accents</h3>
<p>A voice's own accent is a fixed level. TOTAL ACCENT on a step raises every voice on it by the TOTAL ACCENT knob, read when the step plays. With the knob high a voice's accent may not be heard: a normal note at full TOTAL ACCENT is at the accent's level.</p>

<h3 id="scale">Scale and last step</h3>
<p>In pattern write, while it runs:</p>
<ul>
<li><span class="k">SCALE</span> steps 16th, 32nd, 8th triplet, 16th triplet and back, and sets the last step to 16, 16, 12 and 12.</li>
<li><span class="k">LAST STEP</span> held with step key n: the pattern has n steps.</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="shuffle">Shuffle and flam</h3>
<Shot src={panel} base={base} alt="SHUFFLE/FLAM, step keys 1 to 7 and step keys 9 to 16 outlined" crop={lower} marks={[{ ...at.shuffle, n: 1 }, { ...at.shuffleSteps, n: 2 }, { ...at.flamSteps, n: 3 }]} />
<p>In pattern write, while it runs, hold SHUFFLE/FLAM <Num n="1" /> and press:</p>
<ul>
<li><span class="k">Step keys 1 to 7</span> <Num n="2" /> for the shuffle: every second 16th is late by the key's number less one, in 48ths of a beat. 1 is none, 7 is a 75 % swing. In 32nds, 1 and 2, 3 and 4, 5 and 6 are the same. No shuffle on triplets, and an odd last step keeps its length.</li>
<li><span class="k">Step keys 9 to 16</span> <Num n="3" /> for the flam's interval: 16–20 ms at 9 to 44–49 ms at 16, the same at any tempo. The grace note is on the step at normal level, the main note after it at accent level.</li>
</ul>
<p>Held, SHUFFLE/FLAM lights the keys of both settings. A flam's interval takes one of two values about 4 ms apart, from how the step falls against the CPU's scan; the high tom always has the longer.</p>

<h3 id="tap-write">Tap write</h3>
<Shot src={panel} base={base} alt="SHIFT, BACK (TAP), CYCLE/GUIDE, CLEAR and the step keys outlined" crop={lower} marks={[{ ...at.shift, n: 1 }, { ...at.backKey, n: 2 }, { ...at.cycleKey, n: 3 }, { ...at.steps, n: 4 }, { ...at.clear, n: 5 }]} />
<ol>
<li>In pattern write, hold SHIFT <Num n="1" /> and press BACK (TAP) <Num n="2" />. BACK lights instead of TEMPO; SHIFT with TEMPO (STEP) goes back to step write.</li>
<li>Choose the pattern with CLEAR and its step key, and press START.</li>
<li>CYCLE/GUIDE <Num n="3" /> turns the guide on: a rim shot on every beat, louder on the bar's first.</li>
<li>Press a voice's key <Num n="4" /> in time: it is written at the nearest step, accented from the voice's left key, normal from its right one.</li>
</ol>
<p>Held with a voice's key, CLEAR <Num n="5" /> erases that voice as the steps pass, for as long as both are held: with the left key its accented notes, with the right key its normal ones.</p>

<h3 id="clear-copy">Clear, copy, chain</h3>
<ul>
<li><span class="k">Clear:</span> CLEAR with a step key erases that pattern's voices and TOTAL ACCENT, and resets it to 16 steps, 16ths, no shuffle and flam key 13. In EXT INST mode it erases the external instruments and their accent only.</li>
<li><span class="k">Copy:</span> in pattern write, choose the destination with SHIFT, its PATTERN PLAY key and its step key. Then, with SHIFT held: COPY, the source's bank (BANK I or II), its PATTERN PLAY key, its step key, ENTER. Everything of the pattern is copied, from either bank.</li>
<li><span class="k">Chain:</span> in pattern write, hold the step keys at both ends of a run of patterns and press ENTER. Any of them then plays the whole chain from its first. A step key held with ENTER takes its pattern out; the patterns after it stay chained.</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="ext-inst">External instruments</h3>
<p>In pattern write, stopped, SHIFT with EXT INST switches the step keys to 16 external instruments; EXT INST lights. They are written as the voices are, with no flam; INSTRUMENT SELECT with ENTER selects their accent. They play as MIDI notes 36 to 51 on the 909's <a href="#midi-out">MIDI out</a>.</p>
</section>

<!-- ===================================================================== -->
<section id="tracks">
<div class="eyebrow">4</div>
<h2>Tracks</h2>
<p>A track is a list of measures, each one a pattern (its group and number), with a stored tempo. Each bank has four, sharing 896 measures.</p>
<Shot src={panel} base={base} alt="The TRACK PLAY keys, START and STOP/CONT, the display, BACK, FWD and AVAILABLE MEAS, CYCLE, ENTER and the digits under the step keys outlined" crop={lower} marks={[{ ...at.track, n: 1 }, { ...at.startStop, n: 2 }, { ...at.display, n: 3 }, { ...at.fn4, n: 4 }, { ...at.cycleKey, n: 5 }, { ...at.enter, n: 6 }, { ...at.digits, n: 7 }]} />

<h3 id="banks">Banks</h3>
<p>After a TRACK PLAY key, SHIFT with FWD (BANK I) or AVAILABLE MEAS (BANK II) <Num n="4" /> selects the bank; its LED lights. Patterns and tracks are then the bank's.</p>

<h3 id="track-play">Play a track</h3>
<ul>
<li><span class="k">TRACK PLAY 1 to 4</span> <Num n="1" /> selects a track at its first measure; an empty track shows 0 on the display <Num n="3" />.</li>
<li><span class="k">START</span> <Num n="2" /> plays it, and it stops at its end. STOP/CONT stops it and continues.</li>
<li><span class="k">CYCLE</span> <Num n="5" />, its LED lit, repeats it. Pressed again while it plays, the track stops at its end.</li>
<li><span class="k">From a measure:</span> SHIFT with the measure number from the digits <Num n="7" />, then ENTER <Num n="6" />, then STOP/CONT. START still plays from measure 1.</li>
</ul>
<p>The display shows the measure playing, and the tempo while TEMPO is held.</p>

<h3 id="track-write">Write a track</h3>
<ol>
<li>Hold SHIFT and press TRACK PLAY 1 to 4: track write.</li>
<li>To clear the track first: with SHIFT held, DEL, LAST MEAS, ENTER.</li>
<li>Press START.</li>
<li>For each measure, press a PATTERN PLAY key, the pattern's step key, then ENTER. The display shows the next measure's number.</li>
</ol>
<p>Two step keys pressed together enter the patterns between them, a measure each. AVAILABLE MEAS, held, shows the measures left of the bank's 896. When none are left it shows 0, and ENTER shows 0 and adds nothing.</p>

<h3 id="track-edit">Edit a track</h3>
<p>In track write:</p>
<ul>
<li><span class="k">Replace:</span> START, FWD (or ENTER) to the measure, the new pattern, ENTER. BACK steps back a measure. The count stays.</li>
<li><span class="k">Go to a measure:</span> SHIFT with its number, then ENTER.</li>
<li><span class="k">Delete:</span> from that measure, with SHIFT held, DEL, ENTER deletes it; DEL, a number, ENTER deletes up to that measure; DEL, LAST MEAS, ENTER to the end.</li>
<li><span class="k">Insert:</span> go to the measure, select the pattern, and with SHIFT held, INS, ENTER: it goes in before that measure.</li>
<li><span class="k">Copy:</span> go to the destination; with SHIFT held, COPY, the first measure's number, ENTER, the last's, ENTER. A copy of those measures goes in there.</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="tempo-memory">Tempo memory</h3>
<ul>
<li>TEMPO with ENTER stores the tempo in the selected track.</li>
<li>TEMPO with TRACK PLAY 1 to 4 selects the track at its stored tempo; the TEMPO knob then does nothing.</li>
<li>Holding TEMPO and turning the knob gives the tempo back to the knob. The stored tempo stays: TEMPO with the track's key recalls it again, and TEMPO with ENTER replaces it.</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="manual">Where the firmware differs from the manual</h3>
<p>Every procedure in the owner's manual behaves as it says, but for these, which are the version 4.0 firmware's own and are kept:</p>
<div class="scroll">
<table>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Procedure</th><th scope="col">The manual</th><th scope="col">The firmware</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">Track copy of one measure</th><td>"Skip assigning the last measure"</td><td>Nothing is copied</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">CLEAR in tap write</th><td>"3 or 4" for SD</td><td>The left key erases the accented notes, the right key the normal ones</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Erasing the tempo memory</th><td>Erases it</td><td>The knob takes over; the stored tempo stays</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">CLEAR in EXT INST mode</th><td>Scale, last step, shuffle and flam remain</td><td>They remain, and so do the voices</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">MIDI system reset (FF)</th><td>Recognised</td><td>Passed to MIDI out, no other effect</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>

<!-- ===================================================================== -->
<section id="rear">
<div class="eyebrow">5</div>
<h2>The rear</h2>
<p><span class="k">REAR</span> in the bottom bar shows the rear panel.</p>
<Shot src={rear} base={base} alt="The rear panel: cables in the SNARE and BASS DRUM MULTI OUT jacks and in MASTER OUT, the label under each cable, and the POWER switch, outlined" crop={{ x: 0, y: 40, w: 1215, h: 500 }} marks={[{ x: 296, y: 128, w: 456, h: 76, n: 1 }, { x: 194, y: 128, w: 92, h: 76, n: 2 }, { x: 625, y: 497, w: 238, h: 33, n: 3 }, { x: 1074, y: 128, w: 64, h: 62, n: 4 }]} caption="Cables in SNARE and BASS DRUM: the two voices are out of the main mix. Here the DAW has their outputs off." />

<h3 id="multi-out">MULTI OUT</h3>
<ul>
<li><span class="k">A click on a MULTI OUT jack</span> <Num n="1" /> plugs a cable in: as on the unit, its voice leaves the main mix for its own output. A second click pulls it out. The cables are saved with the session.</li>
<li><span class="k">The plugin's outputs:</span> the main pair, MASTER OUT L and R <Num n="2" />, then BD, SD, LT, MT, HT, RS, HC, HH, Crash and Ride. These ten are off until the DAW turns them on, mono or stereo (the jack on both sides).</li>
<li>With no cable, a voice is in the main pair and its output is silent, whatever outputs the DAW turns on. The label under a cable <Num n="3" /> says when the DAW has its output off.</li>
<li>The main pair has the jack board's fixed panning: the low tom and ride left, the high tom and the hi-hats right, the crash a little right. The MULTI OUTs are inverted against it, as on the unit.</li>
</ul>
<p>The other jacks take no cable: MIDI goes through the DAW (see <a href="#midi">MIDI</a>); SYNC IN, START/STOP, TRIG OUT, the tape jacks and the cartridge slot aren't emulated.</p>

<h3 id="power">POWER</h3>
<p>The POWER switch <Num n="4" /> turns the 909 off, silent with the panel dark and its memory kept, and on again. Switching it on is the unit's power-on: the program boots for 0.6 s, the master outputs are muted for about 2 s while C143 charges, and the hi-hat, crash and ride thump at their MULTI OUTs as their stages charge.</p>
<p>A new instance, a session load and State: Reset start as a 909 that has been on a while, playable at once. A session always loads switched on.</p>
</section>

<!-- ===================================================================== -->
<section id="bars">
<div class="eyebrow">6</div>
<h2>The bars and SERVICE</h2>

<h3 id="top-bar">The top bar</h3>
<ul>
<li><span class="k">BETA</span> after the name: this version is a beta. About shows the version.</li>
<li><span class="k">VOICE MODE</span> and a line saying what's missing, while ro-drums-909 runs without its program ROM.</li>
<li><span class="k">ROMs:</span> the ROM folder, the five ROMs and the <a href="#artwork">artwork</a>, with their full paths, and Show, Change… and Rescan for the folder. In voice mode it reads <span class="k">Add Program ROM…</span>: see <a href="#add-rom">Adding ROMs later</a>.</li>
<li><span class="k">State:</span> see <a href="#state">State</a>.</li>
<li><span class="k">About:</span> the version, the ROM folder and artwork in use, the <a href="/support#updates">update check</a>, and links to this guide, the site and the forum. A newer version shows as a button in the bar.</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="state">State</h3>
<Shot src={state} base={base} alt="The State pop-up under the top bar: Save to file…, Load from file… and Reset…" crop={{ x: 455, y: 0, w: 760, h: 240 }} marks={[{ x: 1072, y: 3, w: 66, h: 26, n: 1 }]} caption="State in the top bar." />
<ul>
<li><span class="k">Save to file…</span> saves the instance: the knobs, the pattern and track memory, the MULTI OUT cables, TM and the serial number in SERVICE.</li>
<li><span class="k">Load from file…</span> loads one. The standalone app's Options menu saves and loads the same files.</li>
<li><span class="k">Reset…</span> makes it a new instance: the factory patterns, with the version 1.0 ROM, and every control at its default.</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="bottom-bar">The bottom bar</h3>
<Shot src={panel} base={base} alt="The bottom bar: SYNC, REAR, SERVICE and the status line" crop={{ x: 0, y: 725, w: 760, h: 46 }} marks={[{ x: 10, y: 738, w: 110, h: 26, n: 1 }, { x: 128, y: 738, w: 64, h: 26, n: 2 }, { x: 200, y: 738, w: 81, h: 26, n: 3 }, { x: 290, y: 738, w: 300, h: 26, n: 4 }]} caption="The left end of the bottom bar; ? is at the far right." />
<dl class="legend">
<div><dt><Num n="1" /> SYNC</dt><dd>OFF: the 909 runs on its own clock. HOST: your DAW's transport runs it. See <a href="#sync">Sync</a>.</dd></div>
<div><dt><Num n="2" /> REAR</dt><dd>The rear panel: <a href="#multi-out">MULTI OUT</a> and <a href="#power">POWER</a>.</dd></div>
<div><dt><Num n="3" /> SERVICE</dt><dd>Takes the top panel off to show the boards. See <a href="#service">SERVICE</a>.</dd></div>
<div><dt><Num n="4" /> Status line</dt><dd>The sync mode, INT, MIDI or DIN (HOST while the DAW runs it), and the TEMPO knob's tempo; a missing sample ROM; the MULTI OUTs with a cable in; a key held by a right-click. At the right, the voices sounding.</dd></div>
<div><dt><Num n="5" /> ?</dt><dd>Shows the list of computer keys over the panel. Any click, ? or Escape closes it.</dd></div>
</dl>

<h3 id="service">SERVICE: the boards, TM and the scope</h3>
<Shot src={service} base={base} alt="SERVICE: the voicing board above the switch board, the trimmer TM on the board outlined, and on the right the scope, the readouts, the TM row with TEST and the circuit by serial number" marks={[{ x: 614, y: 296, w: 25, h: 25, n: 1 }, { x: 920, y: 38, w: 288, h: 194, n: 2 }, { x: 920, y: 239, w: 288, h: 59, n: 3 }, { x: 920, y: 303, w: 288, h: 87, n: 4 }, { x: 920, y: 397, w: 288, h: 328, n: 5 }]} caption="SERVICE, the late board selected." />
<p>The voicing board where it hangs under the panel and the switch board folded out in front of it, every part where the service notes' board drawings put it, the copper drawn faintly through. The knobs stay on their pots and the keys on their switches, and work as on the panel; the LEDs and the display run. The wheel or a pinch zooms, and the parts' designators show close up; a drag moves; a double-click shows it all.</p>
<ul>
<li><span class="k">The scope</span> <Num n="2" />: click its caption to clip the probe on a MULTI OUT or master jack, a trigger line, an accent DAC, or any pin of any part the netlists draw, by voice, kind, part and pin. It triggers on a voice's TRIG line, or runs free; time and volts per division are set from their captions. The readouts <Num n="3" /> show its peak to peak, and HAND CLAP LEVEL. While a probe is on, the board doesn't sleep.</li>
<li><span class="k">TM</span>, the hand clap's trimmer (SN p.11), on the board <Num n="1" /> and as a row <Num n="4" />: drag either, shift finer. A double-click puts it back to the calibrated unit's trim, 30 %. TEST sets the scope on the clap's MULTI OUT at the scale of SN p.9 and plays the clap every 0.6 s.</li>
<li><span class="k">Circuit</span> <Num n="5" />: which unit this is, by serial number, from the first units (360100) to the late board (426700 up, the default). Each range has the production changes of SN p.7 up to it: <a href="/ro-drums-909#revisions">Board revisions</a>. Saved with the session.</li>
</ul>
<p>The service notes set TM for 2 Vp-p at the clap's MULTI OUT. At its 30 % the model reads about 4 Vp-p there: its clap's spikes are narrower than the calibrated unit's, and at 2 Vp-p (25.5 %) the clap would be 6 dB under the other voices.</p>
</section>

<!-- ===================================================================== -->
<section id="midi">
<div class="eyebrow">7</div>
<h2>MIDI and your DAW</h2>

<h3 id="midi-notes">MIDI notes</h3>
<p>Notes go into the firmware's own MIDI IN, as on the unit, and play the voices with the firmware's own timing: a hit comes 0.3–2.2 ms after its note.</p>
<div class="scroll">
<table>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Voice</th><th scope="col">Notes</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Bass drum</td><td>36, 35</td></tr>
<tr><td>Snare drum</td><td>38, 40</td></tr>
<tr><td>Low tom</td><td>43, 41</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mid tom</td><td>47, 45</td></tr>
<tr><td>High tom</td><td>50, 48</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rim shot</td><td>37</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hand clap</td><td>39</td></tr>
<tr><td>Closed hat</td><td>42, 44</td></tr>
<tr><td>Open hat</td><td>46</td></tr>
<tr><td>Crash</td><td>49</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ride</td><td>51</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<ul>
<li>Velocity sets the voice's accent level through the firmware's own table: 50 is the sequencer's normal level, 80 its accent. Note-offs are ignored.</li>
<li>The 909 receives on every channel at power-on: see <a href="#channels">MIDI channels</a>.</li>
<li>In voice mode, notes play the voices directly, with the firmware's velocity laws as measured.</li>
<li>MIDI and the sequencer play the same voices.</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="cc">Controllers</h3>
<p>Control changes on any channel move the knobs, on Roland's TR-8 numbers for the same controls. The 909 itself takes none.</p>
<div class="scroll">
<table>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Voice</th><th scope="col">TUNE</th><th scope="col">LEVEL</th><th scope="col">Others</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Bass drum</td><td>20</td><td>24</td><td>ATTACK 21, DECAY 23</td></tr>
<tr><td>Snare drum</td><td>25</td><td>29</td><td>SNAPPY 26, TONE 28</td></tr>
<tr><td>Low tom</td><td>46</td><td>48</td><td>DECAY 47</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mid tom</td><td>49</td><td>51</td><td>DECAY 50</td></tr>
<tr><td>High tom</td><td>52</td><td>54</td><td>DECAY 53</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rim shot</td><td></td><td>57</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>Hand clap</td><td></td><td>60</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>Hi-hat</td><td></td><td>63 or 82</td><td>CH DECAY 62, OH DECAY 81</td></tr>
<tr><td>Crash</td><td>83</td><td>85</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>Ride</td><td>86</td><td>88</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>TOTAL ACCENT</td><td></td><td></td><td>71</td></tr>
<tr><td>VOLUME</td><td></td><td></td><td>7</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<h3 id="midi-out">MIDI out</h3>
<ul>
<li>The plugin's MIDI output is the firmware's MIDI OUT, as on the unit: the external instruments' notes, 36 to 51 on the send channel, and everything received but the clock (the unit's MIDI OUT is a merge).</li>
<li>The 909 never sends its own voices.</li>
<li>Its clock, start, stop and song select aren't sent: the DAW has its own transport.</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="channels">MIDI channels</h3>
<ul>
<li>At power-on the 909 receives on every channel (omni) and sends on 11. A new instance and a session load start there too.</li>
<li>In track play, SHUFFLE/FLAM with step key n sets the receive channel to n, omni off.</li>
<li>In track play, LAST STEP with step key n sets the send channel to n.</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="sync">Sync</h3>
<ul>
<li><span class="k">SYNC: OFF</span> (the default): START, STOP/CONT and TEMPO run the 909's own clock.</li>
<li><span class="k">SYNC: HOST:</span> the 909 goes into its MIDI sync mode, as SHIFT with TEMPO MODE puts it, once the sequencer is stopped. The DAW's transport is sent to it as MIDI clock, start, stop and continue: play starts it at the DAW's tempo, stop stops it. START and STOP/CONT do nothing.</li>
<li>Started from the top of the song, the DAW sends start; from anywhere else, the song position and continue. In track play the 909 goes to that sixteenth of the track, counting each measure at its pattern's length (last step and scale), and plays on from there. In pattern play it ignores the song position and goes on from where the pattern stopped.</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="automation">Automation and sessions</h3>
<p>These parameters can be automated, in this order: BD Tune, BD Level, BD Attack, BD Decay, SD Tune, SD Level, SD Tone, SD Snappy, LT Tune, LT Level, LT Decay, MT Tune, MT Level, MT Decay, HT Tune, HT Level, HT Decay, RS Level, HC Level, HH Level, CH Decay, OH Decay, Crash Level, Crash Tune, Ride Level, Ride Tune, Total Accent, Master Volume, Tempo, Sync to Host and Clap Trim (TM).</p>
<p>A session saves every parameter, the pattern and track memory, the MULTI OUT cables, the serial number in SERVICE and the window size. It loads switched on, as a 909 that has been on a while.</p>
<p>Idle, the voices put out exact zeros and use little CPU, but the CPU keeps running: the sequencer, LEDs and display carry on. ro-drums-909 reports a 2 s tail. Don't let the DAW suspend it on silent tracks, or the 909 can't play its own patterns.</p>
</section>

<!-- ===================================================================== -->
<section id="keyboard">
<div class="eyebrow">8</div>
<h2>Keyboard and mouse</h2>

<h3 id="keys">Computer keys</h3>
<p>Click the panel first. Keys stay down while held, so two-key steps work.</p>
<div class="scroll">
<table>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Keys</th><th scope="col">Panel</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td class="mono">1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8</td><td>Step keys 1 to 8</td></tr>
<tr><td class="mono">Q W E R T Y U I</td><td>Step keys 9 to 16</td></tr>
<tr><td class="mono">A S D F</td><td>TRACK PLAY 1 to 4</td></tr>
<tr><td class="mono">G H J</td><td>PATTERN PLAY 1 to 3</td></tr>
<tr><td class="mono">K</td><td>EXT INST</td></tr>
<tr><td class="mono">Z X C V</td><td>TEMPO (STEP), BACK (TAP), FWD (BANK I), AVAILABLE MEAS (BANK II)</td></tr>
<tr><td class="mono">B N</td><td>CYCLE/GUIDE (LAST MEAS), TAPE SYNC (TEMPO MODE)</td></tr>
<tr><td class="mono">Return</td><td>CARTRIDGE/ENTER (TOTAL ACCENT)</td></tr>
<tr><td class="mono">Shift</td><td>SHIFT, held while the computer's Shift is</td></tr>
<tr><td class="mono">Backspace, Delete</td><td>CLEAR</td></tr>
<tr><td class="mono">F1 F2 F3 F4</td><td>LAST STEP, SCALE, SHUFFLE/FLAM, INSTRUMENT SELECT</td></tr>
<tr><td class="mono">, .</td><td>START, STOP/CONT</td></tr>
<tr><td class="mono">Space</td><td>START, or STOP/CONT while running, in the standalone app (in a DAW, Space is the DAW's)</td></tr>
<tr><td class="mono">?</td><td>The key list</td></tr>
<tr><td class="mono">Escape</td><td>Closes the key list and lets go of every key</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>Some DAWs keep letter keys for themselves.</p>

<h3 id="mouse">Mouse</h3>
<ul>
<li><span class="k">Knobs:</span> drag up or down; shift is finer, double-click resets, the wheel works, hover shows the value.</li>
<li><span class="k">Keys</span> are held while the button is. A right-click (ctrl-click on a Mac) holds one down until it’s clicked again, and the status bar names it; Escape lets go of all.</li>
